  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Stored XSS via news title breaking out of the JSON-LD Article schema",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stored XSS via news title breaking out of the JSON-LD Article schema"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The article title of a news post is embedded **unescaped** into a `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` block on the public news detail page. A title containing `</script>` terminates that script element early, and whatever follows is parsed as HTML — allowing a stored cross-site scripting payload to execute for every visitor who opens the article."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The article title of a news post is embedded **unescaped** into a `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` block on the public news detail page. A title containing `</script>` terminates that script element early, and whatever follows is parsed as HTML — allowing a stored cross-site scripting payload to execute for every visitor who opens the article.\n\nAn attacker who can publish a news article (any admin account) can inject JavaScript that runs in the browser of every reader — students, lecturers, guests, and other administrators — of the affected public page. This enables session/cookie theft (session fixation and DOM access), credential harvesting via injected forms, cryptocurrency mining, defacement, and redirection of victims to attacker infrastructure. Because the payload is persisted server-side, it affects all users without any further interaction from the attacker, and the CSP in place (`script-src 'unsafe-inline'`) does not block it.\n\n1. Encode all values embedded in `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` blocks using `json_encode()` with the `JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT` flags so `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, and `\"` are emitted as `\\uXXXX` escapes and cannot close the script element. Apply this centrally in `app_seo_meta_tags()` so every JSON-LD block benefits.\n2. As defense in depth, HTML-encode the title at storage time (or normalize it) and reject titles that contain `<` or `>` unless legitimately required.\n3. Adopt a strict CSP without `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts (use nonces or hashes) so that any residual inline-handler injection cannot execute.\n4. Add an automated regression test that creates an article with a `</script>` title and asserts the rendered page contains no literal `</script>` inside any `application/ld+json` block.",
                "markdown": "The article title of a news post is embedded **unescaped** into a `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` block on the public news detail page. A title containing `</script>` terminates that script element early, and whatever follows is parsed as HTML — allowing a stored cross-site scripting payload to execute for every visitor who opens the article.\n\nAn attacker who can publish a news article (any admin account) can inject JavaScript that runs in the browser of every reader — students, lecturers, guests, and other administrators — of the affected public page. This enables session/cookie theft (session fixation and DOM access), credential harvesting via injected forms, cryptocurrency mining, defacement, and redirection of victims to attacker infrastructure. Because the payload is persisted server-side, it affects all users without any further interaction from the attacker, and the CSP in place (`script-src 'unsafe-inline'`) does not block it.\n\n1. Encode all values embedded in `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` blocks using `json_encode()` with the `JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT` flags so `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, and `\"` are emitted as `\\uXXXX` escapes and cannot close the script element. Apply this centrally in `app_seo_meta_tags()` so every JSON-LD block benefits.\n2. As defense in depth, HTML-encode the title at storage time (or normalize it) and reject titles that contain `<` or `>` unless legitimately required.\n3. Adopt a strict CSP without `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts (use nonces or hashes) so that any residual inline-handler injection cannot execute.\n4. Add an automated regression test that creates an article with a `</script>` title and asserts the rendered page contains no literal `</script>` inside any `application/ld+json` block."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.4.1"
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stored XSS via news title breaking out of the JSON-LD Article schema\n\nThe article title of a news post is embedded **unescaped** into a `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` block on the public news detail page. A title containing `</script>` terminates that script element early, and whatever follows is parsed as HTML — allowing a stored cross-site scripting payload to execute for every visitor who opens the article."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "helpers/seo_helper.php"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 415,
                  "endLine": 415,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\"><?= json_encode($schema, JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE) ?></script>"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Encode JSON-LD with hex flags so <, >, &, ', \" cannot break out of the script element"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/berita?slug=...",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "helpers/seo_helper.php"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 415,
                        "endLine": 415
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\"><?= json_encode($schema, JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT) ?></script>"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Encode all values embedded in `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` blocks using `json_encode()` with the `JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT` flags so `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, and `\"` are emitted as `\\uXXXX` escapes and cannot close the script element. Apply this centrally in `app_seo_meta_tags()` so every JSON-LD block benefits.\n2. As defense in depth, HTML-encode the title at storage time (or normalize it) and reject titles that contain `<` or `>` unless legitimately required.\n3. Adopt a strict CSP without `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts (use nonces or hashes) so that any residual inline-handler injection cannot execute.\n4. Add an automated regression test that creates an article with a `</script>` title and asserts the rendered page contains no literal `</script>` inside any `application/ld+json` block.",
                "markdown": "1. Encode all values embedded in `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` blocks using `json_encode()` with the `JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT` flags so `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, and `\"` are emitted as `\\uXXXX` escapes and cannot close the script element. Apply this centrally in `app_seo_meta_tags()` so every JSON-LD block benefits.\n2. As defense in depth, HTML-encode the title at storage time (or normalize it) and reject titles that contain `<` or `>` unless legitimately required.\n3. Adopt a strict CSP without `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts (use nonces or hashes) so that any residual inline-handler injection cannot execute.\n4. Add an automated regression test that creates an article with a `</script>` title and asserts the rendered page contains no literal `</script>` inside any `application/ld+json` block."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "cf0af5d4ce12ef482f24b084adeaae63552e766546733485cdd983c52e28cb4f"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-21 23:23:47 UTC",
              "target": "http://172.17.112.1:8001 (DiscipLink) — public news detail page",
              "endpoint": "/berita?slug=...",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An attacker who can publish a news article (any admin account) can inject JavaScript that runs in the browser of every reader — students, lecturers, guests, and other administrators — of the affected public page. This enables session/cookie theft (session fixation and DOM access), credential harvesting via injected forms, cryptocurrency mining, defacement, and redirection of victims to attacker infrastructure. Because the payload is persisted server-side, it affects all users without any further interaction from the attacker, and the CSP in place (`script-src 'unsafe-inline'`) does not block it.",
              "technical_analysis": "## Root cause\n\nThe news title (`judul`) is stored raw. The content sanitizer `NewsController::sanitizeNewsContent()` only sanitizes the **body** (`konten`); the title receives no HTML escaping at storage time.\n\nOn the public detail page, `views/public/berita-detail.php` builds an article schema and calls `app_seo_meta_tags()` with `headline` set to the raw title. That helper renders each schema object with:\n\n```php title=helpers/seo_helper.php startLineNumber=415 endLineNumber=415\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\"><?= json_encode($schema, JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE) ?></script>\n```\n\n`json_encode()` without `JSON_HEX_TAG` / `JSON_HEX_AMP` / `JSON_HEX_APOS` / `JSON_HEX_QUOT` emits `<`, `>`, and `/` literally. Combined with `JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES`, a title containing `</script>` is written verbatim, so the HTML parser closes the `application/ld+json` script element at that point and treats the remaining bytes as markup.\n\nThe `headline` value flows directly from user input:\n\n```php title=views/public/berita-detail.php startLineNumber=113 endLineNumber=128\napp_seo_meta_tags([\n    'title' => (string) ($news['judul'] ?? 'Detail Berita') . ' | DiscipLink',\n    ...\n    'article' => [\n        'headline' => (string) ($news['judul'] ?? ''),\n        ...\n    ],\n]);\n```\n\n## Why the other escaping does not save it\n\n- `<title>`, `<h2>`, breadcrumb, and the OpenGraph/meta tags all pass the title through `htmlspecialchars(ENT_QUOTES)`, so they render safely.\n- The JSON-LD block is the single output context where the title is emitted raw, and it is the one that matters.\n\n## Stored raw, emitted raw\n\nThe record is confirmed stored raw: the admin edit page shows the value only because it is `htmlspecialchars`-encoded there for display. The raw DB value is `<title>zzAREA5XSS</script><svg onload=alert(document.domain)>`.\n\n## CSP does not mitigate\n\nThe response CSP is `script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com https://www.googletagmanager.com`. Because `'unsafe-inline'` is present and no nonce/hash is used, injected inline event handlers (`onload`, `onerror`) execute normally.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Encode all values embedded in `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` blocks using `json_encode()` with the `JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_AMP | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT` flags so `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, and `\"` are emitted as `\\uXXXX` escapes and cannot close the script element. Apply this centrally in `app_seo_meta_tags()` so every JSON-LD block benefits.\n2. As defense in depth, HTML-encode the title at storage time (or normalize it) and reject titles that contain `<` or `>` unless legitimately required.\n3. Adopt a strict CSP without `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts (use nonces or hashes) so that any residual inline-handler injection cannot execute.\n4. Add an automated regression test that creates an article with a `</script>` title and asserts the rendered page contains no literal `</script>` inside any `application/ld+json` block.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate to the admin panel (`/login`, role `admin`).\n2. Create a new article at `/admin/news/tambah`, setting the **title** to the payload `zzAREA5XSS</script><svg onload=alert(document.domain)>` and any non-empty body, then submit (a CSRF token from the same session is included).\n3. Open the resulting public article at `/berita?slug=zzarea5xss-script-svg-onload-alert-document-domain-349`.\n4. Observe that the server response contains the JSON-LD block `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"zzAREA5XSS</script><svg onload=alert(document.domain)>\",...}</script>` — the `</script>` closes the JSON-LD element and the `<svg onload=...>` is parsed as HTML.\n5. In a browser, the injected `<svg>` element is present in the DOM with a live `onload` handler and the JavaScript executes (an `alert` dialog with the page domain is raised; DOM inspection shows the element and its handler).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
